<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomás Estrada Palma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tomas Estrada Palma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomás Estrada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> was a veteran of the Cuban independence struggle who became the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1902, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military government withdrew. His government left a treasury surplus, but his disputed re-election in 1906 provoked a Liberal revolt. He asked the United States to intervene under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a> and resigned, opening the second American occupation of 1906 to 1909.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma.jpg" alt="Tomás Estrada Palma" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Morena A. Loper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Tom%C3%A1s_Estrada_Palma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Estrada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> was born in Bayamo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, the son of Dr Andrés María Estrada y Oduardo and María Candelaria Palma Tamayo. His birth records were lost in a fire in Bayamo Town Hall on 19 January 1869, so the exact date is not known. From 1857 to 1868 he lived in Bayamo as an administrator and local teacher, and later taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and Orange County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He married Genoveva Guardiola Arbizú, daughter of General José Santos Guardiola, President of Honduras, on 15 May 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>He was president of the Cuban Republic in Arms during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a>, was captured by Spanish troops and went into exile. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-marti">José Martí</a>, and after Martí's death became leader of the Cuban Junta and its chief representative. He got the US Congress to pass a joint resolution on 19 April 1898 that disavowed Spanish colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> had no intention of occupying or annexing it. He dissolved the largely black and rural Liberation Army and gave more power to the Assembly of Representatives.</p><p>General Bartolomé Masó withdrew as a candidate in protest against favouritism by the occupation government, and Estrada Palma was elected president on 31 December 1901 without campaigning. He served from 20 May 1902. American troops left after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> lowered tariffs on American goods and wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a> into its constitution. On 16 February 1903 he signed a treaty leasing Guantánamo Bay to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which had wanted five naval bases. His administration built over 328 kilometres of roads; immigration rose from 10,000 in 1902 to 40,000 in 1905, and the Treasury held a few million dollars when he left office.</p><p>His second term was to begin on 20 May 1906. The Liberals opposed it violently, and each side alleged electoral fraud. Estrada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> and the moderate camp appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which began a second occupation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-magoon">Charles Magoon</a>, lasting from 1906 to 1909. On 28 September 1906 he resigned, at 71, with the rest of the executive branch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Estrada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> died of pneumonia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 4 November 1908. A statue of him was put up in 1903 on the Avenida de los Presidentes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. Fidel Castro's revolutionaries pulled it down, reportedly because they blamed him for beginning US interventions, and the plinth with a pair of shoes remains. He is known less for his achievements in education, revolution and infrastructure than for his part in the annexationist agenda and his subservience to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomás Estrada Palma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first president, <b>Tomás Estrada Palma</b>, did not last.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tomás Estrada Palma's</b> disputed re-election in 1906 provoked a Liberal revolt in August.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-cuba">Provisional Government of Cuba</a></li><li><blockquote>Power passed to President <b>Tomás Estrada Palma</b> on 20 May 1902, creating the Republic of Cuba (1902-1959).</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-military-government-in-cuba">United States Military Government in Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bayamo, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. Died in Santiago de Cuba, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.38,-76.64&amp;year=1882" aria-label="Bayamo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2485 1409 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tomás Estrada Palma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2935 1679h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2935 1679h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.38,-76.64&amp;year=1882">What was here in 1882? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
